      "problem": "Find the value of $\\ln\\left(x^3\\right)$ if $\\ln(x) = 0.2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}) & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
